.TH INTRO 3
.SH NAME
intro \- introduction to kernel devices
.SH DESCRIPTION
A kernel device is a tree of files answered inside the kernel by procedure
calls rather than by a program on the far end of a wire.
It still speaks the same protocol as everything else in the tree,
9P2000.L, so a client cannot tell a device from a mounted server by the
messages it sends.
A device's name is
.B #
followed by one character, such as
.BR #c ,
and the character after the
.B #
picks the device out of a table built at boot.
.PP
Access to a device by its
.B #
name reaches it with no namespace at all, which is what a device is for.
A process forked with the
.B RFCNAMEG
flag (see
.IR fork (2))
has an empty name space and no path to anything, and something has to be
able to hand it a console.
.IR root (3)
is the extreme case: the kernel rebuilds a whole name space from
.B #/
after a namespace goes empty, which is the one path back.
Because it bypasses the name space, a
.B #
name is a privilege rather than an ordinary path, and a program is expected
to reach a device through a bound name in its own tree instead.
.IR bind (1)
and
.IR bind (2)
put a device at its conventional place.
.PP
A device registers itself once, at boot, in a table of sixteen slots.
Two devices that want the same character is a build-order mistake, and the
second registration is refused rather than allowed to shadow the first.
The table is consulted on every
.B #
attach and nowhere else, so binding a device where it belongs costs one
lookup and using it afterwards costs none.
.PP
Several files in
.B /dev
are served by one device,
.BR #c .
.IR cons (3),
.IR fb (3),
.IR kbd (3),
.IR uart (3)
and
.IR mouse (3)
each document part of what
.B #c
answers: the console and its control file, the raw screen, the raw scancode
stream, the raw serial line, and the pointer.
A single bind of
.B #c
at
.B /dev
is what makes every one of those files appear together.
.SS The ctl convention
When a device needs an operation that 9P has no message for, the answer is a
file that takes a line of text rather than a new kind of message.
.B /dev/consctl
(see
.IR cons (3))
is the first of these and sets the shape every later one follows.
One write is one command, and a command split across two writes is not
reassembled.
A command nothing recognises is refused whole, with
.BR EINVAL ,
so a client can never believe part of an unrecognised command took effect.
Where reading a
.B ctl
file back is useful, it answers with the commands that would restore the
state it reports, not a status report in a vocabulary of its own.
A last close often reverts what a
.B ctl
file changed, so a program that set a mode and then ended abnormally does
not leave a device stuck in it.
